# The weight p of the choice is a program variable, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p >= 0.  Where p >= 2 the choice is reached with weight p outside [0, 1]: such a
# run is an error from which no bound may be proved, so against a lower bound it is worth 0,
# and the liberal expectation wlp(S, [c = 1]) is 0 there; elsewhere c = 1 and it is 1.  The
# lower bound 1 therefore fails at every p >= 2.
# Expected: unknown.  The cwlp query is reduced to an upper bound on wp(S, 1 - [c = 1]); when an
# out-of-range weight made the run abort it was worth 0 in that reduced wp, that is 1 in wlp,
# as a diverging run is, and the false bound was proved (valid).
nat p;
nat c;
c := 0;
if (p >= 2) {
	{
		c := 1;
	}[p]{
		c := 0;
	}
} else {
	c := 1;
}
?Ex [[c = 1]] >= 1 cwlp
